---
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';
const email = 'hello@lifeatthefrontier.com';
---
<LegalPage
  heading="Editorial standards"
  path="/editorial-standards"
  updated="25 November 2026"
  description="How Life at the Frontier works: independence, evidence, disclosure of interests, how we treat guests and how we correct mistakes."
>
  <p class="lf-doc-lead">
    Life at the Frontier is an independent podcast about the breakthroughs transforming our world, and
    the people behind them. Our listeners trust us to separate what is real from what is promised.
    These are the standards we hold ourselves to.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence first</h2>
  <p>
    Every episode is built around the Frontier Test: what's possible, what actually works, what will
    change, and what it means for you. The second question matters most to us. We look for evidence,
    not announcements, and we make the difference clear:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>We distinguish peer-reviewed research from preprints, company claims and forecasts.</li>
    <li>We say how far along a breakthrough really is, from the lab to everyday life.</li>
    <li>We state uncertainty plainly, and we do not present a guest's projection as fact.</li>
    <li>We check key claims before publication and point to our main sources where we can.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>
    Editorial decisions are ours alone. We choose our subjects, our guests and our questions.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Guests never pay to appear, and appearing is not an endorsement.</li>
    <li>Guests do not approve questions in advance or the final edit.</li>
    <li>
      Any sponsorship or paid partnership is clearly labelled as such. Sponsors have no say over which
      guests we invite, what we ask or what we publish.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Disclosure of interests</h2>
  <p>
    Our host has worked in finance and has founded companies, and that experience shapes the questions
    we ask. It also means we need to be open. If the host has a financial interest, a business
    relationship or a personal connection that is relevant to an episode, a guest or a company we
    discuss, we say so in the episode or its notes. We ask guests to tell us about relevant interests
    too, and we disclose them where they matter to the listener.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing we publish is investment advice. We do not trade on information gained through our work
    before it is public.
  </p>

  <h2>How we treat guests</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We tell guests what the conversation is about and how it will be published.</li>
    <li>
      We edit for length and clarity, never to change the meaning of what someone said or to take it out
      of context.
    </li>
    <li>We challenge claims fairly and give guests the chance to respond.</li>
    <li>Guests can ask us to fix a factual error in what they said before or after publication.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Artificial intelligence</h2>
  <p>
    We may use software tools, including AI, to help with research, transcription and production.
    People make every editorial decision and check everything we publish. We never use AI to invent
    quotes, or to generate or alter the voice or likeness of the host or a guest.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    When we get something wrong, we correct it promptly and openly. Corrections to the website and
    newsletter are noted with the date, and significant errors in an episode are corrected in its notes
    and, where it matters, in a later episode. To report an error, write to
    <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Feedback</h2>
  <p>
    We want to hear from you if you think we have fallen short of these standards. Write to
    <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>. We read every message.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
